 but do NOT invent specific achievements, metrics, or outcomes that have not actually happened\n4. Professional but engaging tone matching the brief\n5. Include source citations [0], [1] if research was provided\n6. NEVER invent contact information - use ONLY the email and phone provided in Official Contact Information above\n7. For body text content, ALWAYS wrap paragraphs in <p> tags - never output raw unwrapped text\n8. For \"content\" fields that contain multiple paragraphs, use proper HTML: <p>Paragraph 1</p><p>Paragraph 2</p>\n9. If contact email or phone is empty in the brief, do NOT make one up - omit it or use a generic \"Contact us\" link\n10. Only create internal links to pages listed in the Internal Linking section above\n11. NEVER invent fake people, client names, or attributed quotes. No \"Sarah Mitchell, Founder\" or \"James Chen, Marketing Director\". If the brief does not include real testimonials, write company philosophy statements instead and leave name/title fields empty\n12. NEVER invent specific statistics, percentages, or metrics. No \"300% increase\" or \"47 minutes\" or \"42-page report\". Describe the type of outcome without fabricating numbers\n13. NEVER invent fake case studies with named businesses. No \"Brighton Physiotherapy Clinic\" or \"Independent Mortgage Broker\" as if they were real clients. Describe service categories and typical outcomes instead\n14. For testimonial sections: write 2-3 statements in the company's own voice about their values, approach, or commitment. These serve as placeholder content the site owner will replace with real testimonials\n15. For case study sections: describe the types of problems the company solves and the approach they take, without inventing specific clients or results\n16. It is ALWAYS better to be honest and general than specific and fabricated. A real visitor will trust \"We help businesses improve their online presence\" far more than a fake testimonial from an invented person\n\nCRITICAL OUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Output ONLY the requested content\n- Do NOT wrap in code fences or markdown formatting\n- Do NOT add preambles like \"Here's what you asked for...\"\n- Do NOT add post-ambles like \"Hope this helps!\"\n- Start directly with the actual content","response_text":"{\n  \"headline\": \"Game Design Consultancy and Co-Development. Built in the UK.\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"You have a game that needs designing, fixing, or shipping. We work with studios, publishers, and developers to solve specific design problems — from systems architecture and economy design to full co-development partnerships. We name the deliverable. We agree the scope. We do the work.\",\n  \"primary_cta\": \"Talk to Us\",\n  \"primary_cta_url\": \"/contact.html\",\n  \"secondary_cta\": \"See How We Work\",\n  \"secondary_cta_url\": \"/services.html\"\n}"}
{"id":"ad417211-961b-448b-8648-6000d1bfb4d0","orchestration_id":"efbc8fb7-d774-4418-9e70-6dfebb4b9f79","prompt_rendered":"Write content for the hero section of Drop Rate Simulator - GameDesign.uk.\n\n## Company Context\nCompany: \nIndustry: \nTone: \nTarget Audience: \nServices: <no value>\nTagline: \n\n## Official Contact Information (USE ONLY THESE - DO NOT INVENT)\nEmail: \nPhone: \nLocation: <no value>\n\n\n## Content Direction (from site spec — follow this closely)\n\nSocial proof style: No fabricated testimonials — the site is a new build with no existing client relationships to cite. Instead, use process credibility: detailed descriptions of how the studio works, named deliverables, and specificity about platforms and tools. When case studies are added later, use challenge-approach-outcome format with concrete metrics where possible. Trust is built through demonstrated knowledge, not claimed endorsements.\n\nVoice: Senior practitioner talking to peers. Direct, specific, and confident without being arrogant. First person plural ('we') when describing the studio's work, second person ('you') when addressing the client's problem. Short sentences. Active voice. Name the actual deliverable, not the abstract benefit. If a section can be said in fewer words, use fewer words. The voice should sound like someone who has shipped games explaining how they work — not a salesperson explaining why you should hire them.\n\nEmphasis: Process confidence — show that the studio knows the steps between concept and shipped product. Platform-specific knowledge — mobile constraints, console certification, cross-platform considerations. Deliverable clarity — every service description should name what the client actually receives. Problem-first framing — lead with the challenge the client faces, then present the studio's approach as the resolution. The UK positioning matters — this is a UK studio for UK and international clients who want a UK partner.\n\nAvoid phrases:\n- We're passionate about\n- Our team of experts\n- Cutting-edge solutions\n- Synergy\n- Holistic approach\n- End-to-end solutions\n- World-class\n- Best-in-class\n- Leverage\n- Innovative\n- Bespoke\n- Turnkey\n- Seamless\n- Take your game to the next level\n- We bring your vision to life\n- In today's fast-paced world\n- Look no further\n- One-stop shop\n\nBlog strategy: Practitioner-written guides targeting specific game design problems. Priority topics for launch: 'How to structure a game design document for small teams', 'Retention mechanics for mobile F2P — what actually works', 'Co-development vs full outsource: how to decide'. Each post should be 1500-2500 words, technically specific, and include at least one framework, template, or decision matrix the reader can use immediately. Posts serve dual purpose: SEO authority building on long-tail terms and demonstration of the studio's expertise to prospective clients who find the site through search. Publish cadence: one post per fortnight minimum at launch, scaling to weekly as the content library grows.\n\n\nTarget Audience: Game studios, indie developers, publishers, and tech companies in the UK seeking design expertise, creative direction, or co-development partnerships for video game and interactive media projects\n\n\n\nImagery Direction: Dark, atmospheric, workspace-focused photography. No stock photos of people smiling at screens. No cartoon game characters. Images should show the artefacts and environments of game design work — system diagrams, wireframes, prototypes on screens, design documents, node graphs. When abstract imagery is used, it should be geometric and structured — grids, networks, flowcharts. Colour in images should be muted with selective accent highlights matching the site palette.\n\n\n\n\n## Section Requirements\nComponent: hero\nFunction: hero\nPurpose: Main hero banner with compelling headline, subheadline, and call-to-action buttons for homepage and landing pages.\n\n\n## Data Schema Required\n{\"fields\": {\"cta_url\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"source\": \"pages.contact\", \"fallback\": \"/contact.html\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"use_fallback\"}, \"cta_text\": {\"type\": \"text\", \"source\": \"llm\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"skip_field\"}, \"headline\": {\"type\": \"text\", \"source\": \"llm\", \"required\": true}, \"subheadline\": {\"type\": \"text\", \"source\": \"llm\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"skip_field\"}, \"secondary_cta\": {\"type\": \"text\", \"source\": \"llm\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"skip_field\"}, \"background_image\": {\"type\": \"image\", \"source\": \"site_assets.hero\", \"fallback\": \"/assets/images/hero.jpg\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"use_fallback\"}, \"secondary_cta_url\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"source\": \"pages.services\", \"fallback\": \"/services.html\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"use_fallback\"}}}\n\n\n\n## Task\nWrite compelling content for this section that is relevant to the company's industry and services.\n\nReturn JSON with these EXACT field names (use the ones that apply to this component type):\n\n### For Hero/Banner sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Your Compelling Main Headline\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Supporting text that expands on the headline\",\n  \"primary_cta\": \"Get Started\",\n  \"primary_cta_url\": \"/contact.html\",\n  \"secondary_cta\": \"Learn More\",\n  \"secondary_cta_url\": \"/about.html\"\n}\n```\n\n### For Feature/Services sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Section Headline\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Brief introduction\",\n  \"features\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Feature Name\", \"description\": \"Feature description\", \"icon\": \"icon-name\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Feature 2\", \"description\": \"Description 2\", \"icon\": \"icon-name\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### For CTA/Call-to-Action sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Ready to Get Started?\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Contact us today\",\n  \"primary_cta\": \"Contact Us\",\n  \"primary_cta_url\": \"/contact.html\"\n}\n```\n\n### For Text/Content/About sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"heading\": \"Section Heading\",\n  \"content\": \"<p>First paragraph of content here.</p><p>Second paragraph here.</p><p>Third paragraph if needed.</p>\"\n}\n```\n\n### For Contact Info sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"heading\": \"Contact Us\",\n  \"email\": \"USE_BRIEF_EMAIL\",\n  \"phone\": \"USE_BRIEF_PHONE\",\n  \"hours\": \"Monday-Friday 9am-5pm\"\n}\n```\n\n### For Testimonial/Social Proof sections:\nUse the company's own voice to describe their approach and values. Do NOT create fake testimonials.\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"What We Stand For\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Our commitment to our clients\",\n  \"testimonials\": [\n    {\"quote\": \"A statement about the company's approach or philosophy in their own voice - NOT attributed to a fake person\", \"name\": \"\", \"title\": \"\"},\n    {\"quote\": \"Another statement about values or working approach\", \"name\": \"\", \"title\": \"\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\nWhen the site owner adds real testimonials later, these placeholders will be replaced.\n\n### For Case Study sections:\nDescribe the types of work the company does and the outcomes they aim for. Do NOT invent specific clients, projects, or results.\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Our Work\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"How we help our clients\",\n  \"case_studies\": [\n    {\"title\": \"Type of project or service area\", \"description\": \"What this involves and what clients can expect\", \"result\": \"The kind of outcomes typically achieved\"},\n    {\"title\": \"Another service area\", \"description\": \"Description of approach\", \"result\": \"Typical outcomes\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n\n\n\n## EXISTING CONTENT — Recreate Mode\nThis page is being adopted from an existing site. Below is the original content from the page.\nYour task for this section: find the content relevant to the \"hero\" section and adapt it to fit the JSON schema above.\n\nPrioritise preserving the original meaning and information. Adapt the structure to match the required JSON field names.\nIf the existing content does not have material relevant to this section, generate fresh content as you normally would.\n\nOriginal page content:\n[GameDesign.uk](https://gamedesign.uk/index.html)\n\n[← Back to Tools](https://gamedesign.uk/index.html)\n\n# Drop Rate Simulator\n\n**Stop trusting your gut on probabilities.** Humans naturally assume that a 1% drop rate means \"I will get one if I kill 100 enemies.\" Mathematically, that is false. In reality, after 100 kills at a 1% drop rate, **37% of your players still won't have the item.**\n\nUse this simulator to visualize the \"Bad Luck\" curve. It helps you decide if your drop rates are fair, or if you need to implement a \"Pity System\" (guaranteed drops) to prevent player churn.\n\n### Loot Config\n\nDrop Chance TypePercentage (%)Fraction (1 in X)\n\nDrop Chance (%)\n\nNumber of Runs (Kills/Opens)\n\nSimulate\n\n#### Chance of Success\n\n92.31%\n\n#### Expected Amount\n\n2.50\n\n#### \"Unlucky\" Players\n\n7.69%\n\n\\*Graph shows the probability of getting exactly X items after the configured runs.\n\n## STRICT RULES:\n1. Use the EXACT field names shown above (headline, subheadline, primary_cta, primary_cta_url, etc.)\n2. No placeholder text like [Your Company] or Lorem ipsum\n3. Write content that is relevant to this company's industry and services - but do NOT invent specific achievements, metrics, or outcomes that have not actually happened\n4. Professional but engaging tone matching the brief\n5. Include source citations [0], [1] if research was provided\n6. NEVER invent contact information - use ONLY the email and phone provided in Official Contact Information above\n7. For body text content, ALWAYS wrap paragraphs in <p> tags - never output raw unwrapped text\n8. For \"content\" fields that contain multiple paragraphs, use proper HTML: <p>Paragraph 1</p><p>Paragraph 2</p>\n9. If contact email or phone is empty in the brief, do NOT make one up - omit it or use a generic \"Contact us\" link\n10. Only create internal links to pages listed in the Internal Linking section above\n11. NEVER invent fake people, client names, or attributed quotes. No \"Sarah Mitchell, Founder\" or \"James Chen, Marketing Director\". If the brief does not include real testimonials, write company philosophy statements instead and leave name/title fields empty\n12. NEVER invent specific statistics, percentages, or metrics. No \"300% increase\" or \"47 minutes\" or \"42-page report\". Describe the type of outcome without fabricating numbers\n13. NEVER invent fake case studies with named businesses. No \"Brighton Physiotherapy Clinic\" or \"Independent Mortgage Broker\" as if they were real clients. Describe service categories and typical outcomes instead\n14. For testimonial sections: write 2-3 statements in the company's own voice about their values, approach, or commitment. These serve as placeholder content the site owner will replace with real testimonials\n15. For case study sections: describe the types of problems the company solves and the approach they take, without inventing specific clients or results\n16. It is ALWAYS better to be honest and general than specific and fabricated. A real visitor will trust \"We help businesses improve their online presence\" far more than a fake testimonial from an invented person\n\nCRITICAL OUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Output ONLY the requested content\n- Do NOT wrap in code fences or markdown formatting\n- Do NOT add preambles like \"Here's what you asked for...\"\n- Do NOT add post-ambles like \"Hope this helps!\"\n- Start directly with the actual content","response_text":"{\n  \"headline\": \"Stop Guessing. See Exactly What Your Drop Rates Do to Players.\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"A 1% drop rate does not mean one item per 100 runs. After 100 attempts, 37% of your players still walk away empty-handed. Simulate your loot tables, visualise the bad luck curve, and decide whether you need a pity system before that design decision ships.\",\n  \"primary_cta\": \"Run the Simulation\",\n  \"primary_cta_url\": \"#simulator\",\n  \"secondary_cta\": \"Back to Tools\",\n  \"secondary_cta_url\": \"/index.html\"\n}"}
{"id":"55ac0645-63c1-44b8-bf48-713369b462f0","orchestration_id":"ea7f87b4-ab3e-495e-814a-3749dee15fd5","prompt_rendered":"Write content for the hero section of Effective Health (EHP) Calculator - GameDesign.uk.\n\n## Company Context\nCompany: \nIndustry: \nTone: \nTarget Audience: \nServices: <no value>\nTagline: \n\n## Official Contact Information (USE ONLY THESE - DO NOT INVENT)\nEmail: \nPhone: \nLocation: <no value>\n\n\n## Content Direction (from site spec — follow this closely)\n\nExample phrases:\nCharacteristic:\n- We design game systems that keep players coming back — and give your team a clear blueprint to build from.\n- From concept to vertical slice, we work alongside your studio to shape the experience before a single line of code is written.\n- Good game design isn't magic. It's craft, iteration, and knowing what players actually need.\n- We've shipped titles on mobile, PC, and console. We know what it takes to get a game over the line.\n- Tell us about your project. We'll tell you honestly what it needs.\nWould never say:\n- We are passionate about delivering innovative gaming solutions tailored to your unique needs.\n- Our world-class team of experts will transform your vision into reality.\n- Limited availability — book your consultation slot today before they're gone!\n- We leverage cutting-edge technology to create bespoke, holistic game experiences.\n- As a leading game design agency, we offer end-to-end synergistic development pipelines.\n\nParagraph style:\nStructure: One idea per paragraph. Open with the most important point, support briefly, move on. Don't bury the lead. Use bullet points for lists of three or more items rather than cramming them into prose.\nTypical length: 2–4 sentences per paragraph. Games industry readers are often time-poor and scan-read. Dense paragraphs lose them. White space is part of the design.\n\nVoice:\nAuthority level: Establishes authority through demonstrated knowledge of game development pipelines, platforms, and creative challenges — not through credentials lists. Shows expertise by how it talks about problems, not just by claiming to solve them.\nPerson: Second person (you/your) when addressing prospective clients; first plural (we/our) when describing the studio or team. Avoid passive constructions.\nRegister: Confident and craft-focused — speaks as a practitioner who lives in the games industry, not a generalist agency talking about it from the outside. Matter-of-fact about capability, enthusiastic about the work.\nFormality: Professional but direct. Uses contractions naturally. Avoids corporate filler language. Writes the way a senior creative at a respected studio would talk in a pitch meeting.\nEmotional tone: Energised and purposeful — conveys genuine passion for games as a craft without tipping into fan-culture hyperbole. Serious about quality, approachable about process.\n\nCta style:\nApproach: CTAs should feel like a natural next step, not a pressure tactic. Frame them around what the reader gets from acting, not what the company wants. Primary CTAs are project-focused; secondary CTAs offer lower-commitment engagement (view work, read case study).\nVerb choices: Start a project, Get in touch, See our work, Talk to us, View case study, Discuss your game — avoid 'Submit', 'Click here', 'Learn more' (too generic), or anything with urgency pressure like 'Act now'.\n\nSentence style:\nStructure patterns: Lead with the verb or the outcome. 'We design games that…' not 'Our approach involves the design of…'. Use active voice throughout. Vary sentence length deliberately — short sentences land key points, longer ones build context.\nRhythm: Energetic but controlled. Three-beat structures work well for lists of capabilities. Avoid monotonous sentence-after-sentence uniformity — the rhythm should feel like a well-paced game level, not a policy document.\nAverage length: Short to medium — punchy declarative sentences for impact, with occasional longer sentences when explaining process or context\n\nThings to avoid:\n- The word 'passionate' and its variants — overused to the point of being a red flag in games industry copy\n- Generic agency language: 'innovative solutions', 'bespoke offerings', 'holistic approach'\n- Unverified superlatives: 'the best', 'world-class', 'leading' without substantiation\n- Fan-culture language that makes the studio sound like a hobbyist rather than a professional partner\n- Walls of text on service pages — the audience scans, not reads linearly\n- Urgency or scarcity language ('limited slots', 'act now') — undermines professional credibility\n- Listing tools and engines without explaining what they enable for the client\n- Overpromising on timelines or outcomes without qualifying context\n\nThings to emulate:\n- Lead every service description with the client problem it solves, not the company capability\n- Use specific platform, genre, and engine references to signal real-world expertise\n- Structure case studies around challenge → approach → outcome for maximum credibility\n- Match the rhythm of copy to the energy of the work — games are dynamic; the writing should feel alive\n- Use white space and short paragraphs to give the content room to breathe, mirroring good UI design\n- Name the deliverable at the end of every service — clients need to know what they're buying\n- Reference the UK games industry context to anchor the studio's relevance and market awareness\n- Show process confidence — describe how work gets done, not just what gets done\n\nPersuasion approach:\nTrust building: Trust in the games industry is built through specificity — naming platforms, genres, engines, and pipelines signals that this is real expertise. Reference real challenges the audience faces (tight production timelines, platform compliance, player retention) to demonstrate insider understanding.\nSocial proof style: Testimonials should be specific and outcome-focused — from named studios or publishers where possible. Avoid generic praise quotes. Project case studies with visible before/after or challenge/solution framing are more credible than star ratings.\nMethod: Show, don't tell. Let project outcomes, process descriptions, and specific capability language do the persuading. Avoid superlatives. Instead of 'world-class design', describe what the design actually achieves for players.\n\nTerminology:\nApproach: Use industry-standard terminology without explanation for a professional audience. Introduce the studio's own process terminology (if any) on first use with a brief clarification. Never use jargon as a substitute for clear thinking.\nKey terms:\n- Game design document (GDD)\n- Player experience (PX)\n- Core loop\n- Mechanics and systems\n- Platform compliance\n- Co-development\n- Vertical slice\n- Onboarding\n- Retention\n- Shipping / shipped title\n\nContent depth:\nThoroughness: Service pages should go one level deeper than competitors — explain the 'how' briefly, not just the 'what'. Homepage and hero sections stay high-level. Inner pages earn the right to go into process detail.\nAssumed knowledge: Audience understands game development broadly — they know what a GDD is, what 'shipping' means, what Unity and Unreal are. Don't explain basic industry terms. Do explain the studio's specific approach or methodology.\nExplanation pattern: Introduce a challenge the client faces → describe the approach taken → describe the outcome achieved. This three-part pattern works for service descriptions, case studies, and about copy alike.\n\nHeading style:\nFormat: Direct and outcome-oriented. H1s should state what the company does or delivers. H2s should name the service or section clearly. Avoid clever wordplay that obscures meaning. 'Game Design That Ships' is better than 'Where Ideas Become Reality'.\nHierarchy: H1: one per page, states the core proposition. H2: section titles that organise content logically. H3: used for individual services, team members, or case study sub-sections. Never use headings purely for visual weight — every heading should be scannable and informative.\n\nWriting rules:\n- Never use the word 'passionate' — it is overused in the games industry to the point of meaninglessness. Show passion through specificity instead.\n- Always name platforms, genres, or engines when relevant — 'mobile game for iOS and Android' is stronger than 'mobile game'.\n- Avoid the phrase 'cutting-edge' — instead, name the specific technology or technique that makes something current.\n- When describing a service, always include what the client gets at the end — a deliverable, a capability, a shipped product.\n- Use game design vocabulary naturally: mechanics, systems, player experience, loop, onboarding, retention — but only where accurate, not as decoration.\n- Every page should have one clear action the reader can take next — don't leave them at a dead end.\n- Write project descriptions in past tense with concrete outcomes; write service descriptions in present tense as active offers.\n- Avoid vague scale claims like 'hundreds of projects' unless substantiated — specificity beats volume in this industry.\n- When referencing the UK games industry context, tie it to a client benefit — not just a market stat for its own sake.\n- Headings should be functional and specific — they should tell the reader what the section delivers, not tease it cryptically.\n\n\nTarget Audience: Game studios, indie developers, publishers, and tech companies in the UK seeking design expertise, creative direction, or co-development partnerships for video game and interactive media projects\n\n\n\nImagery Direction: High-quality screenshots, concept art, and in-development assets where available. Abstract game-world visuals or stylised geometric compositions as backgrounds where photography isn't appropriate. Imagery should feel like it belongs in an art book or press kit — curated and intentional, not stock. Show the work; avoid generic 'people at computers' imagery.\n\n\n\n\n## Section Requirements\nComponent: hero\nFunction: hero\nPurpose: Main hero banner with compelling headline, subheadline, and call-to-action buttons for homepage and landing pages.\n\n\n## Data Schema Required\n{\"fields\": {\"cta_url\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"source\": \"pages.contact\", \"fallback\": \"/contact.html\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"use_fallback\"}, \"cta_text\": {\"type\": \"text\", \"source\": \"llm\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"skip_field\"}, \"headline\": {\"type\": \"text\", \"source\": \"llm\", \"required\": true}, \"subheadline\": {\"type\": \"text\", \"source\": \"llm\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"skip_field\"}, \"secondary_cta\": {\"type\": \"text\", \"source\": \"llm\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"skip_field\"}, \"background_image\": {\"type\": \"image\", \"source\": \"site_assets.hero\", \"fallback\": \"/assets/images/hero.jpg\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"use_fallback\"}, \"secondary_cta_url\": {\"type\": \"url\", \"source\": \"pages.services\", \"fallback\": \"/services.html\", \"required\": false, \"on_missing\": \"use_fallback\"}}}\n\n\n\n## Task\nWrite compelling content for this section that is relevant to the company's industry and services.\n\nReturn JSON with these EXACT field names (use the ones that apply to this component type):\n\n### For Hero/Banner sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Your Compelling Main Headline\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Supporting text that expands on the headline\",\n  \"primary_cta\": \"Get Started\",\n  \"primary_cta_url\": \"/contact.html\",\n  \"secondary_cta\": \"Learn More\",\n  \"secondary_cta_url\": \"/about.html\"\n}\n```\n\n### For Feature/Services sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Section Headline\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Brief introduction\",\n  \"features\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Feature Name\", \"description\": \"Feature description\", \"icon\": \"icon-name\"},\n    {\"name\": \"Feature 2\", \"description\": \"Description 2\", \"icon\": \"icon-name\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### For CTA/Call-to-Action sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Ready to Get Started?\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Contact us today\",\n  \"primary_cta\": \"Contact Us\",\n  \"primary_cta_url\": \"/contact.html\"\n}\n```\n\n### For Text/Content/About sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"heading\": \"Section Heading\",\n  \"content\": \"<p>First paragraph of content here.</p><p>Second paragraph here.</p><p>Third paragraph if needed.</p>\"\n}\n```\n\n### For Contact Info sections:\n```json\n{\n  \"heading\": \"Contact Us\",\n  \"email\": \"USE_BRIEF_EMAIL\",\n  \"phone\": \"USE_BRIEF_PHONE\",\n  \"hours\": \"Monday-Friday 9am-5pm\"\n}\n```\n\n### For Testimonial/Social Proof sections:\nUse the company's own voice to describe their approach and values. Do NOT create fake testimonials.\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"What We Stand For\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Our commitment to our clients\",\n  \"testimonials\": [\n    {\"quote\": \"A statement about the company's approach or philosophy in their own voice - NOT attributed to a fake person\", \"name\": \"\", \"title\": \"\"},\n    {\"quote\": \"Another statement about values or working approach\", \"name\": \"\", \"title\": \"\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\nWhen the site owner adds real testimonials later, these placeholders will be replaced.\n\n### For Case Study sections:\nDescribe the types of work the company does and the outcomes they aim for. Do NOT invent specific clients, projects, or results.\n```json\n{\n  \"headline\": \"Our Work\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"How we help our clients\",\n  \"case_studies\": [\n    {\"title\": \"Type of project or service area\", \"description\": \"What this involves and what clients can expect\", \"result\": \"The kind of outcomes typically achieved\"},\n    {\"title\": \"Another service area\", \"description\": \"Description of approach\", \"result\": \"Typical outcomes\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n\n\n\n## EXISTING CONTENT — Recreate Mode\nThis page is being adopted from an existing site. Below is the original content from the page.\nYour task for this section: find the content relevant to the \"hero\" section and adapt it to fit the JSON schema above.\n\nPrioritise preserving the original meaning and information. Adapt the structure to match the required JSON field names.\nIf the existing content does not have material relevant to this section, generate fresh content as you normally would.\n\nOriginal page content:\n[GameDesign.uk](https://gamedesign.uk/index.html)\n\n[← Back to Tools](https://gamedesign.uk/tools/index.html)\n\n# Effective Health (EHP) Calculator\n\n**Health is a lie.** 1,000 HP with 50% damage reduction is effectively 2,000 HP. This is your **Effective Health Pool (EHP)**.\n\nUse this tool to balance Tanks vs. Glass Cannons. Check if it's better to buff a unit's Armor or their raw Hitpoints.\n\n### Unit Stats\n\nRaw Health (HP)\n\n* * *\n\nCalculation MethodFlat Percentage (e.g. 25% Resist)MOBA Formula (Armor / (Armor + K))\n\nDamage Reduction (%)\n\nArmor Value\n\nScaling Constant (K)Standard: WoW (K=10500), LoL (K=100)\n\nEvasion / Dodge Chance (%)\n\nCalculate Durability\n\n#### Effective Health (EHP)\n\n2,857\n\nTrue damage needed to kill\n\n#### Total Mitigation\n\n30.0%\n\n#### EHP Multiplier\n\n1.43x\n\n1 HP = X EHP\n\n\\*Graph compares Raw HP vs. Physical EHP vs. EHP with Dodge\n\n## STRICT RULES:\n1. Use the EXACT field names shown above (headline, subheadline, primary_cta, primary_cta_url, etc.)\n2. No placeholder text like [Your Company] or Lorem ipsum\n3. Write content that is relevant to this company's industry and services - but do NOT invent specific achievements, metrics, or outcomes that have not actually happened\n4. Professional but engaging tone matching the brief\n5. Include source citations [0], [1] if research was provided\n6. NEVER invent contact information - use ONLY the email and phone provided in Official Contact Information above\n7. For body text content, ALWAYS wrap paragraphs in <p> tags - never output raw unwrapped text\n8. For \"content\" fields that contain multiple paragraphs, use proper HTML: <p>Paragraph 1</p><p>Paragraph 2</p>\n9. If contact email or phone is empty in the brief, do NOT make one up - omit it or use a generic \"Contact us\" link\n10. Only create internal links to pages listed in the Internal Linking section above\n11. NEVER invent fake people, client names, or attributed quotes. No \"Sarah Mitchell, Founder\" or \"James Chen, Marketing Director\". If the brief does not include real testimonials, write company philosophy statements instead and leave name/title fields empty\n12. NEVER invent specific statistics, percentages, or metrics. No \"300% increase\" or \"47 minutes\" or \"42-page report\". Describe the type of outcome without fabricating numbers\n13. NEVER invent fake case studies with named businesses. No \"Brighton Physiotherapy Clinic\" or \"Independent Mortgage Broker\" as if they were real clients. Describe service categories and typical outcomes instead\n14. For testimonial sections: write 2-3 statements in the company's own voice about their values, approach, or commitment. These serve as placeholder content the site owner will replace with real testimonials\n15. For case study sections: describe the types of problems the company solves and the approach they take, without inventing specific clients or results\n16. It is ALWAYS better to be honest and general than specific and fabricated. A real visitor will trust \"We help businesses improve their online presence\" far more than a fake testimonial from an invented person\n\nCRITICAL OUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Output ONLY the requested content\n- Do NOT wrap in code fences or markdown formatting\n- Do NOT add preambles like \"Here's what you asked for...\"\n- Do NOT add post-ambles like \"Hope this helps!\"\n- Start directly with the actual content","response_text":"{\n  \"headline\": \"Know Your Real Durability\",\n  \"subheadline\": \"Raw HP doesn't tell the whole story. The EHP Calculator shows you how much true damage it actually takes to kill a unit — factoring in damage reduction, armor formulas, and dodge chance. Balance your tanks, tune your glass cannons, and design combat that holds up under pressure.\",\n  \"primary_cta\": \"Calculate EHP\",\n  \"primary_cta_url\": \"/contact.html\",\n  \"secondary_cta\": \"Back to Tools\",\n  \"secondary_cta_url\": \"/tools/index.html\"\n}"}
{"id":"adc8b5df-32ba-4dca-a649-b86f8f92a8d9","orchestration_id":"ff47d18d-34b6-44d7-8554-078ca7b73001","prompt_rendered":"Write content for the hero section of TTK Calculator - GameDesign.uk.\n\n## Company Context\nCompany: \nIndustry: \nTone: \nTarget Audience: \nServices: <no value>\nTagline: \n\n## Official Contact Information (USE ONLY THESE - DO NOT INVENT)\nEmail: \nPhone: \nLocation: <no value>\n\n\n## Content Direction (from site spec — follow this closely)\n\nExample phrases:\nCharacteristic:\n- We design game systems that keep players coming back — and give your team a clear blueprint to build from.\n- From concept to vertical slice, we work alongside your studio to shape the experience before a single line of code is written.\n- Good game design isn't magic. It's craft, iteration, and knowing what players actually need.\n- We've shipped titles on mobile, PC, and console. We know what it takes to get a game over the line.\n- Tell us about your project. We'll tell you honestly what it needs.\nWould never say:\n- We are passionate about delivering innovative gaming solutions tailored to your unique needs.\n- Our world-class team of experts will transform your vision into reality.\n- Limited availability — book your consultation slot today before they're gone!\n- We leverage cutting-edge technology to create bespoke, holistic game experiences.\n- As a leading game design agency, we offer end-to-end synergistic development pipelines.\n\nParagraph style:\nStructure: One idea per paragraph. Open with the most important point, support briefly, move on. Don't bury the lead. Use bullet points for lists of three or more items rather than cramming them into prose.\nTypical length: 2–4 sentences per paragraph. Games industry readers are often time-poor and scan-read. Dense paragraphs lose them. White space is part of the design.\n\nVoice:\nAuthority level: Establishes authority through demonstrated knowledge of game development pipelines, platforms, and creative challenges — not through credentials lists. Shows expertise by how it talks about problems, not just by claiming to solve them.\nPerson: Second person (you/your) when addressing prospective clients; first plural (we/our) when describing the studio or team. Avoid passive constructions.\nRegister: Confident and craft-focused — speaks as a practitioner who lives in the games industry, not a generalist agency talking about it from the outside. Matter-of-fact about capability, enthusiastic about the work.\nFormality: Professional but direct. Uses contractions naturally. Avoids corporate filler language. Writes the way a senior creative at a respected studio would talk in a pitch meeting.\nEmotional tone: Energised and purposeful — conveys genuine passion for games as a craft without tipping into fan-culture hyperbole. Serious about quality, approachable about process.\n\nCta style:\nApproach: CTAs should feel like a natural next step, not a pressure tactic. Frame them around what the reader gets from acting, not what the company wants. Primary CTAs are project-focused; secondary CTAs offer lower-commitment engagement (view work, read case study).\nVerb choices: Start a project, Get in touch, See our work, Talk to us, View case study, Discuss your game — avoid 'Submit', 'Click here', 'Learn more' (too generic), or anything with urgency pressure like 'Act now'.\n\nSentence style:\nStructure patterns: Lead with the verb or the outcome. 'We design games that…' not 'Our approach involves the design of…'. Use active voice throughout. Vary sentence length deliberately — short sentences land key points, longer ones build context.\nRhythm: Energetic but controlled. Three-beat structures work well for lists of capabilities. Avoid monotonous sentence-after-sentence uniformity — the rhythm should feel like a well-paced game level, not a policy document.\nAverage length: Short to medium — punchy declarative sentences for impact, with occasional longer sentences when explaining process or context\n\nThings to avoid:\n- The word 'passionate' and its variants — overused to the point of being a red flag in games industry copy\n- Generic agency language: 'innovative solutions', 'bespoke offerings', 'holistic approach'\n- Unverified superlatives: 'the best', 'world-class', 'leading' without substantiation\n- Fan-culture language that makes the studio sound like a hobbyist rather than a professional partner\n- Walls of text on service pages — the audience scans, not reads linearly\n- Urgency or scarcity language ('limited slots', 'act now') — undermines professional credibility\n- Listing tools and engines without explaining what they enable for the client\n- Overpromising on timelines or outcomes without qualifying context\n\nThings to emulate:\n- Lead every service description with the client problem it solves, not the company capability\n- Use specific platform, genre, and engine references to signal real-world expertise\n- Structure case studies around challenge → approach → outcome for maximum credibility\n- Match the rhythm of copy to the energy of the work — games are dynamic; the writing should feel alive\n- Use white space and short paragraphs to give the content room to breathe, mirroring good UI design\n- Name the deliverable at the end of every service — clients need to know what they're buying\n- Reference the UK games industry context to anchor the studio's relevance and market awareness\n- Show process confidence — describe how work gets done, not just what gets done\n\nPersuasion approach:\nTrust building: Trust in the games industry is built through specificity — naming platforms, genres, engines, and pipelines signals that this is real expertise. Reference real challenges the audience faces (tight production timelines, platform compliance, player retention) to demonstrate insider understanding.\nSocial proof style: Testimonials should be specific and outcome-focused — from named studios or publishers where possible. Avoid generic praise quotes. Project case studies with visible before/after or challenge/solution framing are more credible than star ratings.\nMethod: Show, don't tell. Let project outcomes, process descriptions, and specific capability language do the persuading. Avoid superlatives. Instead of 'world-class design', describe what the design actually achieves for players.\n\nTerminology:\nApproach: Use industry-standard terminology without explanation for a professional audience. Introduce the studio's own process terminology (if any) on first use with a brief clarification. Never use jargon as a substitute for clear thinking.\nKey terms:\n- Game design document (GDD)\n- Player experience (PX)\n- Core loop\n- Mechanics and systems\n- Platform compliance\n- Co-development\n- Vertical slice\n- Onboarding\n- Retention\n- Shipping / shipped title\n\nContent depth:\nThoroughness: Service pages should go one level deeper than competitors — explain the 'how' briefly, not just the 'what'. Homepage and hero sections stay high-level. Inner pages earn the right to go into process detail.\nAssumed knowledge: Audience understands game development broadly — they know what a GDD is, what 'shipping' means, what Unity and Unreal are. Don't explain basic industry terms. Do explain the studio's specific approach or methodology.\nExplanation pattern: Introduce a challenge the client faces → describe the approach taken → describe the outcome achieved. This three-part pattern works for service descriptions, case studies, and about copy alike.\n\nHeading style:\nFormat: Direct and outcome-oriented. H1s should state what the company does or delivers. H2s should name the service or section clearly. Avoid clever wordplay that obscures meaning. 'Game Design That Ships' is better than 'Where Ideas Become Reality'.\nHierarchy: H1: one per page, states the core proposition. H2: section titles that organise content logically. H3: used for individual services, team members, or case study sub-sections. Never use headings purely for visual weight — every heading should be scannable and informative.\n\nWriting rules:\n- Never use the word 'passionate' — it is overused in the games industry to the point of meaninglessness. Show passion through specificity instead.\n- Always name platforms, genres, or engines when relevant — 'mobile game for iOS and Android' is stronger than 'mobile game'.\n- Avoid the phrase 'cutting-edge' — instead, name the specific technology or technique that makes something current.\n- When describing a service, always include what the client gets at the end — a deliverable, a capability, a shipped product.\n- Use game design vocabulary naturally: mechanics, systems, player experience, loop, onboarding, retention — but only where accurate, not as decoration.\n- Every page should have one clear action the reader can take next — don't leave them at a dead end.\n- Write project descriptions in past tense with concrete outcomes; write service descriptions in present tense as active offers.\n- Avoid vague scale claims like 'hundreds of projects' unless substantiated — specificity beats volume in this industry.\n- When referencing the UK games industry context, tie it to a client benefit — not just a market stat for its own sake.\n- Headings should be functional and specific — they should tell the reader what the section delivers, not tease it cryptically.\n\n\nTarget Audience: Game studios, indie developers, publishers, and tech companies in the UK seeking design expertise, creative direction, or co-development partnerships for video game and interactive media projects\n\n\n\nImagery Direction: High-quality screenshots, concept art, and in-development assets where available. Abstract game-world visuals or stylised geometric compositions as backgrounds where photography isn't appropriate. Imagery should feel like it belongs in an art book or press kit — curated and intentional, not stock. Show the work; avoid generic 'people at computers' imagery.\n\n\n\n\n## Section 